\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In contrast to the effective elastic or plastic properties obtained in classical micromechanics, it is presently not established in general that fracture in a heterogeneous material gives rise to a homogenized macroscopic material model with a well-defined fracture toughness obtained in an RVE limit.}}{2}{section*.16}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To avoid conflating two slightly different concepts, the term \textsl {effective crack resistance} is used in this work for the value obtained from a numerical experiment under specific less general conditions, i.e., the maximum energetic driving force required to force crack advance through the considered microstructure. In contrast, \textsl {fracture toughness} is reserved for a generalized material quantity in the sense of a macroscopic fracture property. Whether such a fracture toughness exists for heterogeneous materials and under which circumstances both concepts might coincide remains an open question of research.}}{2}{section*.17}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{propose the effective fracture energy to be a directionally dependent quantity that corresponds to the dissipated energy of the actual path of a crack through a suitable unit cell, leading to full fracture of that unit cell in a given direction. The authors also show that this definition yields a rigorous lower bound to the effective fracture energy.}}{3}{section*.18}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{assess essentially the same variational definition and discuss its merits and limitations. In particular, they relate the variational surface energy to limit-load problems and show that it provides a lower bound to a more physically motivated effective fracture energy based on the energy dissipated along the actual crack path through a unit cell.}}{3}{section*.19}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Recent contributions underline that this remains an active field of research. Liu and Shen\nobreakspace {}\cite {liu_effective_2025} propose a phase-field formulation for anisotropic effective fracture toughness that accounts for a quasi-worst-case translation of a representative volume element. Aranda and Segurado\nobreakspace {}\cite {aranda_cracklength_2025} introduce a crack-length control technique for phase-field fracture in FFT homogenization and use it to estimate effective fracture properties of composites and porous materials. Man et al.\nobreakspace {}\cite {man_collaborative_2025} study how crack guiding and trapping in bioinspired interfaces control effective toughness, while Henry\nobreakspace {}\cite {henry_phasefield_2025} shows for dynamic crack propagation in disordered heterogeneous materials that the spatial distribution of soft inclusions can strongly affect the apparent fracture energy even when the effective elastic properties are similar.}}{3}{section*.20}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective fracture toughness}}{3}{section*.21}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective crack resistance}}{3}{section*.22}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The methodology is not used here as a proof of a homogenized fracture toughness in the mathematical sense. Rather, it is interpreted as a numerical counterpart of a fracture experiment. In this experiment-like setting, a representative material region containing microstructural features is subjected to so-called surfing boundary conditions that enforce complete separation, and the energetic driving force required for this process is recorded.}}{3}{section*.23}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A recent related development is the variable stiffness boundary condition of Hao et al.}}{3}{section*.24}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{, which builds on the surfing boundary condition but translates a simple uniform remote displacement into a non-uniform load through an engineered stiffness gradient, thereby facilitating implementation in experiments and commercial finite-element software.}}{3}{section*.25}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{such a}}{3}{section*.26}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{a}}{3}{section*.27}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ of phase-field models for fracture}}{3}{section*.28}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For ductile materials, phase-field fracture has also been coupled to plasticity in variational finite-strain formulations, for example by Miehe, Aldakheel and co-workers}}{3}{section*.29}%
