\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{, i.e., during characteristic crack growth through the representative region}}{7}{section*.78}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The index $c$ denotes the critical crack-resistance value obtained from this maximum, whereas the index $x$ is retained to avoid implying isotropic crack resistance.}}{7}{section*.79}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This definition should not be confused with a proof that $J_{x,c}^{\text {eff}}$ converges to a material property under enlargement of the representative region. Establishing such convergence, if it exists for a given class of microstructures and loading paths, would require a separate study.}}{7}{section*.80}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For the numerical experiment introduced by Hossain et al.}}{7}{section*.81}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{, two formulations of the surfing boundary conditions were compared in}}{7}{section*.82}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{. For the specific cases investigated there, including the formulation used in the present work, no relevant difference in the predicted value of $J_{x,c}^{\text {eff}}$ was found. This should not be understood as a general proof of independence from the boundary conditions.}}{7}{section*.83}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This presumes a sufficiently homogeneous spatial distribution of microstructural features, analogous to assumptions made for representative volume elements in micromechanics.}}{7}{section*.84}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{This presumes a sufficiently homogeneous spatial distribution of microstructural features, analogous to assumptions made for representative volume elements in micromechanics.}}{7}{section*.85}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In the present work, this requirement is not fully verified: only one prescribed macroscopic crack-growth direction and one initial crack location are considered, and no convergence study with respect to the size of the resolved porous region is performed. The results should therefore be read as a controlled numerical experiment on the influence of J2-plasticity for the selected simplified microstructure, rather than as the determination of a generally valid homogenized fracture toughness.}}{7}{section*.86}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A phase field model of ductile fracture}}{7}{section*.87}%
