\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{.}}{7}{section*.92}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The notation and numerical treatment of the underlying small-strain $J_2$ plasticity model follow standard computational plasticity concepts; see, for example, Simo and Hughes}}{8}{section*.93}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{.}}{8}{section*.94}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ that is related to the density of dislocations in the crystallographic microstructure}}{8}{section*.95}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Irreversibility of crack growth is enforced in both the ductile and the linear elastic phase-field formulations by fixing the phase field at fully fractured points: if $s(\boldsymbol {x},t_{\boldsymbol {x}})=0$ for the first time $t_{\boldsymbol {x}}$ at a point $\boldsymbol {x}$, then a Dirichlet condition $s(\boldsymbol {x},t)=0$ is imposed for all later pseudo-times $t>t_{\boldsymbol {x}}$.}}{9}{section*.96}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{when}}{9}{section*.97}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{if}}{9}{section*.98}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The cubic degradation function is used because it allows the prescribed yield stress and hardening parameter to be implemented in the phase-field formulation in a direct manner.}}{9}{section*.99}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{discuss that, for the standard quadratic degradation function, these parameters require a reinterpretation in order to obtain effective material properties that can be related to measured data.}}{9}{section*.100}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Finally, the fracture energy contribution is given by}}{9}{section*.101}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Finally, the fracture energy density per unit volume is given by}}{9}{section*.102}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Here, $\bar {A}$ approximates the crack surface, $J_c$ denotes the material-specific fracture energy per unit crack surface area, and $\epsilon $ controls the width of the diffuse crack zone.}}{9}{section*.103}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Here, $\bar {A}$ approximates the crack surface upon integration over a volume, $J_c$ denotes the material-specific fracture energy per unit crack surface area, and $\epsilon $ controls the width of the diffuse crack zone.}}{10}{section*.104}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For the linear elastic case, it can be shown that the zero set of $s$ minimizing the total energy converges to minimizers obtained with a sharp crack representation as $\epsilon \to 0$, see\nobreakspace {}\cite {chambolle_approximation_2004}.}}{10}{section*.105}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For the linear elastic case, Ambrosio--Tortorelli-type regularizations approximate the sharp-crack variational formulation in the sense of $\Gamma $-convergence as $\epsilon \to 0$. In this sense, the regularized fields $(\boldsymbol {u},s)$ approximate the displacement field and crack set of the corresponding sharp-crack formulation, while the diffuse crack region represented by low values of $s$ approaches the crack set; no pointwise convergence statement for $s$ is intended here, see Kuhn\nobreakspace {}\cite {kuhn_numerical_2013} and Chambolle\nobreakspace {}\cite {chambolle_approximation_2004}.}}{10}{section*.106}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective fracture toughness}}{10}{section*.107}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective crack resistance}}{10}{section*.108}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ e.g. described in \nobreakspace {}\cite {kuhn_continuum_2010}}}{10}{section*.109}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ described, e.g., by\nobreakspace {}\cite {kuhn_continuum_2010}}}{10}{section*.110}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The weak forms of the coupled boundary-value problems are implemented in FEniCSx using \texttt {dolfinx} version 0.7.4}}{11}{section*.111}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{. The displacement field, the phase field, and, where applicable, the plastic internal variables are solved in a monolithic nonlinear system. The nonlinear residual and its Gateaux derivative are assembled and solved with the PETSc-based Newton solver provided by FEniCSx. The linear subproblems arising within Newton's method are solved with the default PETSc Krylov solver GMRES and the default PETSc ILU preconditioner used by this setup.}}{11}{section*.112}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The evolution equation for the phase field, Eq.\nobreakspace {}(\ref {eq:evolution_s}), is discretized in pseudo-time by an implicit backward Euler scheme. The time step is adapted automatically during the simulation. The maximum number of Newton iterations per time step is set to eight. If a step does not converge within this limit, the time step is reduced by a factor of two and the step is repeated. If convergence is reached in fewer than four Newton iterations, the next time step is increased by a factor of two, subject to the prescribed upper bound for the respective simulation.}}{11}{section*.113}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The spatial discretization uses first-order Lagrange finite elements on triangular meshes in two dimensions and tetrahedral meshes in three dimensions. Along the expected crack path, the mesh is locally refined such that the element edge length satisfies $\Delta h \leq \epsilon /2$ and is usually chosen much smaller; see the respective sections for the specific values. The specific values of $\Delta h$ and $\epsilon $ used in the individual simulations are stated together with the corresponding numerical setups below.}}{11}{section*.114}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective fracture resistance}}{11}{section*.115}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{effective crack resistance}}{11}{section*.116}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The parameter set [\textbf {Eq$\boldsymbol {J_c}$}]\xspace is chosen to have the same prescribed microscopic fracture resistance $J_c$ as [\textbf {vM}]\xspace , whereas [\textbf {Eq$\boldsymbol {\sigma ^*}$}]\xspace is chosen such that the one-dimensional tensile strength is close to that of [\textbf {vM}]\xspace .}}{11}{section*.117}%
